With $c=1$, the Schwarzschild line element is $\begin{aligned}
ds^2={}&-\left(1-\frac{2GM}{r}\right)dt^2
+\frac{dr^2}{1-\frac{2GM}{r}}
+r^2d\Omega_2^2,\\
d\Omega_2^2={}&d\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2 .
\end{aligned}$ The Schwarzschild radius is $R_s=2GM$, or $R_s=2GM/c^2$ when $c$ is restored. Far away, $r\gg R_s$, both radial coefficients approach their flat-space values. At $r=R_s$, however, the coefficient of $dt^2$ vanishes and the coefficient of $dr^2$ diverges. That looks dramatic. The question is whether the geometry is dramatic or only the coordinates.

