p.src:
The Sun has mass $M_\odot\sim10^{30}\,\mathrm{kg}\sim10^{38}m_P$. Cubing and multiplying by one Planck time gives the lecture’s deliberately rough chain $t_{\mathrm{evap}}\sim10^{114}t_P
\sim10^{71}\ {\rm s}
\sim10^{64}\ {\rm yr}.$ Either estimate is fantastically longer than the roughly $10^{10}$-year age of the universe. Restoring the coefficient in Equation  raises the standard solar-mass estimate to about $2\times10^{67}$ years.

